# Browser automation — tool profiles

`context/instructions/browser-tools.md` profiles the browser-automation drivers
that fit SDT's protocol (`context/instructions/browser.md`) and states how to
choose between them. The profiles are **defaults, not requirements**: a project
may use any driver with the same capabilities, and the choice is recorded in its
own `context/instructions/project.md` (library-first, see
`context/instructions/development.md`).

## How to choose

- **Layout truth** (pixel geometry, computed styles, both color schemes) → a
  real-engine driver. `agent-browser` is the profiled default.
- **Read-only extraction / eval / parallel scrape** (no layout assertion) → a
  self-contained engine. `obscura` is the profiled default.
- **Neither present** → degrade: report the render step as **SKIPPED** (see
  `browser.md`), never PASSED; do not install a driver unprompted.
- **CLI over MCP when both exist** → prefer a CLI invocation: it is more
  token-efficient than an MCP tool, because it avoids loading a large tool schema
  and verbose accessibility trees into context (`playwright-cli` @ `b85c7a7`).
  MCP stays useful for long-running loops that need persistent state and rich
  introspection.
- Both drivers share the wait-by-outcome discipline and the
  pixel-diff-is-a-tripwire rule from `browser.md`.

## `agent-browser` (Vercel Labs) — default for layout truth

- Shape: a Rust CLI over a native daemon that drives **real Chrome/Chromium via
  CDP**; the daemon auto-starts, persists between commands and idle-times out.
- Agent-first interface: `snapshot` returns a compact accessibility tree with
  stable refs; `screenshot --annotate` overlays numbered labels that map 1:1 onto
  refs; `diff snapshot|screenshot|url` gives structural/pixel diffs with a
  threshold and dimension-mismatch detection.
- Verification primitives: `set viewport` / `set device` / `--color-scheme`,
  `get box`, `get styles`, `a11y` (embedded axe-core, no CDN), `vitals`
  (LCP/CLS/TTFB/FCP/INP), `record`.
- Security controls (opt-in): allowed domains, per-category action policy,
  confirmation prompts, output cap, content boundaries.
- Costs: a Chromium download and a persistent daemon — set an idle timeout and
  run `doctor` before blaming the tool.

## `obscura` (Apache-2.0) — default for read-only extraction

- Shape: a self-contained Rust engine with **no Chromium**; it owns the DOM,
  layout and paint and runs JS through V8. It ships a CLI, a CDP server and an
  MCP server.
- Interface: `fetch --dump html|text|links|markdown|assets|original|cookies`,
  `--eval`, `--screenshot`, `--wait-until`, parallel `scrape`, `serve`.
- Its own docs state that long-tail CSS, some Web APIs, media and platform font
  rasterization may differ from Chromium: it is **not a layout oracle**.
- Fit: quick content dumps, resource/SSR debugging, cheap checks and parallel
  extraction before deciding a real browser session is needed.

## `page-agent` (Alibaba) — text-only, in-page

- Shape: an agent embedded **in the page** (in-page JavaScript) that acts on a
  **text DOM**; no screenshots, no multi-modal model and no headless browser, and
  it brings its own LLM (`@9eb6b66`).
- Fit: text-only navigation and form work when a real-engine driver is
  unavailable. It is **not a layout oracle**: it cannot measure pixel geometry,
  so for a layout claim the render step stays **SKIPPED** (see `browser.md`).
- Costs: it runs inside the page it controls, so it cannot inspect a page it did
  not author and it shares that page's origin and permissions.

## Version scope

- Bind to the installed driver, not to this profile: run the tool's own help or
  CLI-served skills (`--help`, `skills get ...`) and re-verify flags before
  relying on them. Both tools evolve quickly; this file is a starting point, not
  a frozen contract.
- No install recipe lives here. A project that adopts a driver records how it is
  installed and invoked in its own `context/instructions/project.md`.
